"ACT Completes Formation of Public Benefit Corporation and Partnership with Nexus Capital Management (https://industryinsights.act.org/2024/05/act-completes-formation-partnership-nexus)\nciteturn51search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; May 01, 2024 ... IOWA CITY, Iowa — ACT, Inc., the mission-driven provider of the assessments, research, and work-ready credentials designed to support education and workplace success, today announced that it has completed its formation of a new Delaware public benefit corporation and closed on its partnership with Nexus Capital Management, a Los Angeles-based private equity firm.\n\n# ACT Completes Formation of Public Benefit Corporation and Partnership with Nexus Capital Management\n\nMay 01, 2024\n\nACT\n\nCompany will transform college and career pathways for students, educators, and employers\n\n \n\nIOWA CITY, Iowa — ACT, Inc., the mission-driven provider of the assessments, research, and work-ready credentials designed to support education and workplace success, today announced that it has completed its formation of a new Delaware public benefit corporation and closed on its partnership with Nexus Capital Management, a Los Angeles-based private equity firm.\n\n \n\nJanet Godwin will continue as CEO of the new company, which will be known as ACT Education Corp. (temporarily known as Impact Asset Corp.). The new public benefit corporation combines ACT and Encoura, an education data science and research organization that was a wholly owned subsidiary of ACT. ACT Education Corp. will maintain all of its current offerings, including its flagship ACT college and career readiness assessment, and continue to offer its readiness assessments, enrollment insights, and workforce credential solutions to its thousands of partners globally.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDaily Iowan (Iowa City, Iowa), 1970-06-25 (https://dailyiowan.lib.uiowa.edu/DI/1970/di1970-06-25.pdf)\nciteturn51search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.5 years ago; Crawled: 13.8 years ago; To capture the daily life of a group of people known for their insistence on clinging to “old-fashioned” ways, Zielinski moved into an Amish settlement near Kalona, Iowa, in 1967. ... West Branch 643-5687, John Wentz. 6-2<br><br>\nTHE DAILY IOWAN — Iowa City, Ia. — Thurs., Dec. 25, 1970 — Page 1\n\n# Photographer Captures Iowa Life\n\n## Some Photographs . . .\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nLarge black-and-white photo of an American flag in the foreground with a courthouse-like building and dome in the background.\n</visual_element>\n\nArticle text at upper right:\n\nAn Iowa photographer will contribute something a little different to Iowa City this week with a “store-front” photography exhibit.\n\nJohn M. Zielinski, a 1968 graduate of the University of Iowa, will exhibit some 150 photographs at 128 Clinton Street, for a brief stay starting Friday.\n\nZielinski, now a graduate student in the University Art Department, is best known for his photographic documentary on the Iowa Amish. A touring exhibit of his Amish work, sponsored by the Iowa Arts Council, opens Monday at the Lake Okoboji Art Center. This exhibit, “People of the Soil” has previously been shown at centers in Davenport, Cedar Rapids, and Des Moines. Other Zielinski exhibits have been seen in Hartford, Conn., and Cologne, Germany.\n\nAnother exhibit of his work is currently being displayed at Kirkwood Community College, Cedar Rapids.\n\nHis exhibit here will be held from 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. Friday, Saturday and Tuesday, and from noon until 8 p.m. Monday. Zielinski spent approximately three years preparing his series of photographs dealing with Iowa’s Amish.\n\nTo capture the daily life of a group of people known for their insistence on clinging to “old-fashioned” ways, Zielinski moved into an Amish settlement near Kalona, Iowa, in 1967. He devoted nearly seven months to photographing the Amish, before his most recent postcards are from pictures he took during last month’s campus demonstrations in Iowa City.\n\nZielinski’s photographs are primarily done in black and white; the use of these “non-colors” can, he thinks, express Amish life more humanly and with greater honesty. He has adopted the style of the 1930’s Farm Security Administration photographers, a group Zielinski admittedly has used as his “masters.”\n\nZielinski is now back among the Amish, near Kalona, Iowa, and is working on a project which combines current photographs of Iowa with old pictures covering more than a century of Iowa history.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nLarge black-and-white photo of a crowd outdoors in front of a building; caption below reads “... And Zielinski, with Friends”.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e3\">\nBlack-and-white portrait-style photo of John M. Zielinski indoors holding photographs, standing in front of a wall display of framed pictures.\n</visual_element>\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n~.f~:::::::::::::::::~::::~:::::::::::::::!I.::~'::::::::::::::::::::::::~)~:::::::::::;;;:;:;:::::::;:;:::::;:;:;:;:~;:~;:::'! (https://dailyiowan.lib.uiowa.edu/DI/1972/di1972-03-06.pdf)\nciteturn51search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; John and Mary \nZielinski \nAmish ice-cutting is as much a social affair as an economic one ... Highway 1 from Iowa City and\nTtl. Dilly IOWln-lOWI City, lowl-Mon., Mlrell', 1m-Pitt 5 \n• I \nPhotos and story \nby \nJohn and Mary \nZielinski \nAmish ice-cutting is as much a social affair as an economic one \nWinter cools Amish iceboxes \ncommunity who live near \nK~lona. \nsupplies three or four icehouses \n(all located on Amish farms) \nused by more than one family. \nWith a full load, there's only \none place for the driver: braced \nstraddling the ice In the wallon. \nThe initial culting is done with \na large, gas-powered saw moun-\nted on wooden sled and pushed \nalong by ha