way in 1940. Educated in the schools of Des Moines county, and Central University of Pella, Iowa. Married Ora Westral of Yarmouth, Iowa, in 1912, and the father of three sons: Jack of Yarmouth, James Newton, and the oldest son, Plon, is deceased. Principal occupation has been farming, but has been engaged in numerous community activities. Served on the Yarmouth consolidated school board for eighteen years, on the county board of supervisors for twelve years, president of the Fifth District county officials for four years, and president of the state association of Boards of Supervisors. Member of the Farm Bureau, A.F. & A.M., Kaaba Shrine and Elks. Serving second term.\n\nOPPEDAHL, Edward, Renwick  \nHumboldt County\n\nBorn in Humboldt County on a farm four miles south of Renwick, Iowa, May 2, 1890. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLetter to Membership on Acquisition of ACT by Nexus Capital Management – All Access | College Board (https://allaccess.collegeboard.org/letter-membership-acquisition-act-nexus-capital-management)\nciteturn51search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Letter to Membership on Acquisition of ACT by Nexus Capital Management\n\nCollege Board Communications April 10, 2024\n\n# Letter to Membership on Acquisition of ACT by Nexus Capital Management\n\nCollege Board Communications April 10, 2024\n\nMany of you have asked for our perspective on ACT’s announcement today that it has been acquired by Nexus Capital Management, a private equity firm based in Los Angeles. It’s unclear what this purchase will mean for ACT and the students it currently serves. What is clear is that ACT will now operate as a for-profit company. \n\nAt College Board, as a member-led, not-for-profit organization, our mission remains the same. From innovations like the digital SAT and BigFuture to our day-to-day operations, our work is driven by member and educator input and an unwavering focus on students. That will not change.\n\nWe have long supported students having options as they prepare for college and career. We have worked with ACT for many years on issues of common concern. We expect that to continue. \n\nThank you, as always, for your partnership.\n\nJeremy Singer\nPresident, College Board --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n~r~~:;;~:;:;:;:;;;~:::~:::::::~:::':~;;;:;:;:::;:;;;:;~:;:;:~\"::'.'::~::::::;::~::~:~:;;X;;;:;W1 (https://dailyiowan.lib.uiowa.edu/DI/1972/di1972-04-17.pdf)\nciteturn51search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; heart of the Iowa Amish com· ... To John Zielinski, the Amish\nIon-MoL, AprIl 11, Irn-Paget \nr \nStrange I \n,I , \n[ \nTheir lives are tied to the ea1~th \ninto the twentieth century, \nmeans \"no pictures.\" \nPhotos by John Zielinski. \nZielinski's Amish work have \nbeen featured in a number of \npublications including Ufe, \nPhotographic Society of \nAmerica Journal, Famous \nPhotographers Magazlae and \nInfinity. \nNearly seven years ago, a \nyoung photographer decided to \nrecord this other way of life and \nset up living in an unheated, \n$4O-a-month farmhouse in the \nheart of the Iowa Amish com· \nmunity just outside the samll \ntown of Kalona. \ni l \nOn Friday his portfolio book, \nThe Amish: People of the Soli, \nwas published under the \nPhoto·Art imprint. It consists of \n40 black and white reproduc-\ntions of the most representative \nof his Amish work. \nBetween that time and now, \nZielinski produced a fuU record \nof the Amish way of life that in \n1968 received a grant from the \nIowa Arts Council to become a \ntouring photographic exhibit. \nIts reception was such that in-\nstead of the original one year, \nthe show toured for nearly two \nand then, at the suggestion of \nArts Council \ne¢or J\\:\\Qk \nOlds, Zielinski fol \ned it with a \nsecond documentary show on \nthe Mesquakie Indians at \nTama. \nIn between the Amish exhibit \nand the Indian one (which has \nbeen toured by the council since \nI alit. June) , \nse~ectlons from \nCurrently a graduate student \nat the University of Iowa, \nZielinski hopes to have a second \nportfoliO issued early next year \non the Indians. \nTo John Zielinski, the Amish \nwere no strangers and no mere \nanachronism from the past: \nthey represented a lasting \nemobidment of the pioneer \nspirit, something that, as a mid-\nwesterner, he appreciated and \nwas determined to record \nbefore the inevitable march of \nprogress eradicated it. \nIn an age of machine·bred \nalienation and freaked-out con· \nformity. the plain, simple \npeople known as the Amish \nstand out like a signpost poin-\nting backward to when life was \ntied to the earth. \nPacifist long before it was \nfashionable, these people have \nretained a pioneer way of life \nthat few outsiders have ever \nreally understood or portrayed. \nUsually they are photographed \nmore as curiosities or as ad-\nmirable examples of an earlier \nlife that is no longer practicable \nor thinkable. The task of \ndocumenting these people is \nmade doubly difficult by their \nreligious admonition of \"no \ngraven image\" which, carried \nI \nI I ' .1 \nI \nI \nI \nI \n\"I [ \nI I I I I \n45.00 I \nI I I I I I I 11 \nI ..--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTable 4: Per capita GDP: Africa, Asia, and World Averages, 1960-2023 (https://publicadministration.desa.un.org/sites/default/files/The%20critical%20role%20of%20political%20commitment%2C%20transformational%20leadership%2C%20and%20human%20resources%20in%20governance%20innovation%20-%20an%20African%20perspective_0.pdf)\nciteturn51search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Table 4: Per capita GDP: Africa, Asia, and World Averages, 1960-2023 ... By 1990, the global per capita GDP on average reached US$7,170, while Africa’s average was US$1,458, representing 20.3 per cent of global average.\nTable 4: Per capita GDP: Africa, Asia,